    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/david-vii-ulu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-vii-ulu.jpg" alt="David VII Ulu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/david-vii-ulu">David VII Ulu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_David_VII_Ulugh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">13th century artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Imereti, the land of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> lying beyond the Likhi Range around the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>, had been the heart of the old Kingdom of Abkhazia and a core region of the united Kingdom of Georgia. Its separate history as a kingdom began during the Mongol domination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. In the 1240s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> recognised two cousins as co-kings of Georgia: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-vii-ulu">David VII Ulu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-vi-narin">David VI Narin</a>, son of Queen Rusudan. In 1259–1260 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Narin joined a rising against Mongol overlordship and, when it failed, withdrew west to Kutaisi, where the Mongols could not easily reach him. He ruled western Georgia as an independent king until his death in 1293, and his descendants succeeded him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> the Brilliant reunited the western kingdom with the rest of Georgia in 1330. During the crisis caused by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>’s invasions in the 1380s, descendants of David Narin revived a separate western kingdom for a time, before the kings of Georgia reasserted their authority. The decisive break came in the fifteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrat-iii-of-georgia">Bagrat</a>, a prince of a junior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagratid</a> line who governed in the west, made himself king of Imereti, defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-viii-of-georgia">George VIII</a> at the Battle of Chikhori in 1463 and took Kartli as well in 1465. After the council of 1490 recognised the division of Georgia, Imereti remained a separate kingdom under his descendants.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kutaisi.jpg" alt="Kutaisi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Kutaisi_%26_White_Bridge_as_seen_from_Mt_Gora_(August_2011)-cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kober, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Imereti was a land of hills, river valleys and forests with a humid climate, and its economy rested on farming and stock-raising. Peasants grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and wheat and tended vineyards, and from the seventeenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, introduced from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, gradually became the staple food of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Society was hierarchical. The princely families, or <em>tavadi</em>, and the lesser nobility, or <em>aznauri</em>, held land and peasants in a system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>, and the church and great monasteries such as Gelati were major landowners. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a> was the only significant town, a royal seat and market rather than a large commercial city. Trade ran mainly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> ports controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and their clients, and the most notorious export was people. Western Georgian nobles sold captives and even their own serfs to Ottoman slave traders, and raiding for slaves between rival princes depopulated parts of the country. European visitors, including the French traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-chardin">Jean Chardin</a>, who passed through western Georgia in the 1670s, described the trade and the poverty that accompanied it. Religious life centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a>, whose western branch was headed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicate-of-abkhazia">Catholicos of Abkhazia</a>, and Catholic missionaries also worked in the region in the seventeenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bagrat-iii-of-georgia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bagrat-iii-of-georgia.jpg" alt="Bagrat III of Georgia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bagrat-iii-of-georgia">Bagrat III of Georgia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bagrat_III_of_Georgia_(Bedia_mural).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom’s armed forces consisted of the levies of nobles and their retainers, summoned by the king in time of war, and were rarely large or well equipped. Fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shorapani">Shorapani</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagdati">Bagdati</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartikeya">Skanda</a> guarded the passes and river routes, and control of them was often the decisive issue in wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrat-iii-of-georgia">Bagrat III</a> (r. 1510–1565) was one of the most active kings: he fought the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a>, defeating them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-murjakheti">Murjakheti</a> in 1535, but a coalition of Georgian rulers was beaten by the Ottomans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sokhoista">Sokhoista</a> in 1545. Much of Imereti’s fighting was internal, against its own great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-dadiani">Dadiani of Mingrelia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gurieli">Gurieli of Guria</a> and the dukes of Racha, or against the eastern Georgian kingdoms, and these wars often drew in Ottoman or Persian intervention. Ottoman garrisons held key fortresses for long periods, including the fortress of Kutaisi. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-i-of-imereti">Solomon I</a> won the kingdom’s best-known victory when he defeated an Ottoman army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chikhori-1757">Battle of Chikhori</a> in 1757. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1768–1774, a Russian force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottlob-curt-heinrich-von-tottleben">Gottlieb Totleben</a> campaigned in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> took the opportunity to drive Ottoman garrisons from fortresses in his kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/archil-of-imereti"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/archil-of-imereti.jpg" alt="Archil of Imereti" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/archil-of-imereti">Archil of Imereti</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artschillus_Bagarationus._Koningh_van_Iberia_en_Melita.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicolaes Witsen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Imereti was ruled by a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a>, whose kings were crowned at Gelati or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a> and buried in the Gelati monastery, the kingdom’s royal shrine. In theory the king was overlord of all western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, including the principalities of Mingrelia, Guria and Abkhazia and the mountain region of Svaneti. In practice these <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> became independent in all but name during the sixteenth century, and the kingdom proper was reduced to Imereti itself, where even the dukes of Racha often defied the crown. Royal government was simple: the king relied on a small court, on princely families who held hereditary offices and on the church. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-amasya">Treaty of Amasya</a> of 1555 between the Ottoman and Safavid empires placed western Georgia in the Ottoman sphere, and Imereti paid tribute to the sultans for over two centuries, sometimes including captives. Successions were frequently contested, and in the late seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries kings were made and unmade by noble factions, the Ottoman pasha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhaltsikhe">Akhaltsikhe</a> and the eastern Georgian kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archil-of-imereti">Archil</a>, a cultivated poet who ruled Imereti several times between the 1660s and 1690s, ended his life in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-i-of-imereti">Solomon I</a> (r. 1752–1784) strengthened royal authority, curbed the nobility and in the 1750s prohibited the sale of slaves.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bagrati-cathedral"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bagrati-cathedral.jpg" alt="Bagrati Cathedral" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bagrati-cathedral">Bagrati Cathedral</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2014_Kutaisi,_Katedra_Bagrati_(19).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marcin Konsek, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For more than three centuries the Kingdom of Imereti preserved an independent Georgian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monarchy in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, on the frontier between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. It maintained the traditions of the medieval kingdom: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a> remained a royal capital, and the Gelati monastery, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia">David IV the Builder</a>, continued to receive royal burials, endowments and new wall paintings. The church of Gelati and the remains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrati-cathedral">Bagrati Cathedral</a>, damaged by an Ottoman explosion in 1692, are today among Georgia’s most important monuments. Imereti also shaped the political geography of western Georgia. The struggle between the kings and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in Mingrelia, Guria and Abkhazia produced the separate principalities that survived into the Russian period, and the Imeretian dialect and regional identity remain distinct within modern Georgia. The alliance between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-i-of-imereti">Solomon I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II of Kartli-Kakheti</a> in the eighteenth century, and their shared turn towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, prepared the incorporation of Georgia into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. After annexation, the kingdom’s lands formed the core of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi-governorate">Kutaisi Governorate</a>, and Kutaisi grew into Georgia’s second city. Members of the royal family continued in Russia as the princes Imeretinsky.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/solomon-i-of-imereti"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solomon-i-of-imereti.jpg" alt="Solomon I of Imereti" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/solomon-i-of-imereti">Solomon I of Imereti</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solomon_I_of_Imereti_(National_museum_of_Georgia).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Surprizi, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Imereti’s final decline came as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> expanded into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kucuk-kaynarca">Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca</a> of 1774, which ended the Russo-Turkish war, freed Imereti from the tribute in captives owed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, but the kingdom remained poor and divided. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-i-of-imereti">Solomon I</a> died in 1784, his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ii-of-imereti">David II</a> struggled with rival claimants, and in 1789 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-ii-of-imereti">Solomon II</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II of Kartli-Kakheti</a>, took the throne with eastern Georgian support. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> annexed Kartli-Kakheti in 1801, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> II, under pressure, signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-elaznauri">Treaty of Elaznauri</a> in 1804, accepting Russian protection while keeping his throne. He resisted Russian demands for direct control and sought help from the Ottomans and the pasha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhaltsikhe">Akhaltsikhe</a>. In 1810 Russian troops occupied Imereti and deposed him. Solomon was taken prisoner, escaped to Ottoman territory and died in exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> in 1815. Russia abolished the kingdom and placed Imereti under military administration. Popular resentment broke out in a major rising in 1819–1820, provoked partly by Russian reforms of the western Georgian church, which had recently lost its own Catholicos and been placed under the Russian-controlled exarchate. The rising was suppressed, and Imereti became a province of the Russian Empire.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Imereti preserved a Georgian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monarchy in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> for more than three centuries after the break-up of the medieval kingdom, surviving Ottoman overlordship, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> and rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> before Russian annexation in 1810 brought it into the empire that absorbed all of Georgia.</em></p>

